From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Feder <noreply@splitmind.invalid>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 11:08:14 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] feat(connections): per-connection read-only setting

OOMOL_CONNECT_READ_ONLY_CONNECTIONS lists <providerId>:<connectionName>
entries, separated by commas or whitespace. A listed connection refuses
every call that is not a read, for every caller:

- Actions (MCP execute_action and POST /v1/actions/:actionId, both through
  ActionRunner): only operationType "read" runs. "write", "destructive" and
  a missing operationType are refused with action_blocked and a deployment
  policy check, and the refusal is recorded in the run log.
- POST /v1/proxy/:service: every request is refused with 403, whatever the
  method, because a raw HTTP request cannot be safely classed as a read.
- Triggers: reconcile and resource (they create or delete provider
  webhooks) are refused with 403. read, options, source and receive still
  work, and cleanup and maintenance still remove existing webhooks.
- Guidance agrees with enforcement (#94): when a connection is selected
  (get_action_guide connectionName, the default connection for
  search_actions, ?connectionName=, ?alias= or x-oo-connector-alias on the
  console agent.md route), the capability policy and agent.md report a
  write, destructive or unlabelled action as not allowed, with the same
  message and deployment check as execute_action. Both call the same rule
  (applyReadOnlyToDecision, built on refuseAction). With no connection
  selected, output is unchanged.
- search_actions applies the rule only when the caller's grant allows the
  default connection, so it agrees with execute_action's "not granted".
- The email watch's ConnectionService gets the read-only list too.
- Entries that can never match (uppercase provider id, "*", a provider not
  in the catalog) are kept and warned about once each.

list_connections and /v1/apps/services/:service add readOnly: true to
listed connections and omit the field otherwise. Matching is exact and
case-sensitive. Malformed entries are ignored with one warning each. Unset
or empty changes nothing.
